# Design principles

Apply DRY, KISS, and SOLID on every change. Choose the smallest design that still gives each module one reason to change.

## DRY

One concept has one home. Call that home from every game, screen, and store.

```typescript
formatLapTime(lap.timeMs) // screens call this; they do not format times themselves
```

## KISS

Ship the straight path. Add a layer when a second caller needs it. A function that checks one lap and returns it is enough until two callers share that check.

## SOLID

- **S** — Decoding a packet, deciding a lap counts, and saving it live in separate modules.
- **O** — A new game year arrives as a new decoder beside the old one.
- **L** — A stand-in decoder can replace the real one in tests and still return a lap.
- **I** — The live screen asks for speed, throttle, and brake. It does not receive the UDP socket.
- **D** — Storage is used through a small repository. Screens never open the database.
